<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menorca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Minorca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Menorca, or Minorca, is the second-largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a>. It is rich in Talaiotic monuments, including T-shaped taulas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naveta-des-tudons">Naveta des Tudons</a>. Under Muslim rule it survived as a tributary of Aragon after 1231 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-aragon">Alfonso III of Aragon</a> conquered it in 1287, and it was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for most of the 18th century until it passed finally to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1802.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menorca.jpg" alt="Menorca" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_de_Menorca.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
